problems.problem:
(0.25 for each correct answer) Measuring the distances of the stars is fundamental in astronomy and Cepheids make it possible. A Cepheid-type star is a yellow giant or supergiant star, with 4 to 15 times the mass of the Sun and 100 to 30000 times the luminosity (= power) of the Sun. The luminosity of Cepheids varies over a well-defined period, between 1 and 100 days. The name "Cepheid" comes from the pulsating star Delta Cephei (in the constellation of Cepheus), whose variability of apparent brightness was discovered in 1784.

A relation was discovered between the pulsation period ($P$) of the Cepheid and its absolute magnitude ($M_v$), given by $M_v = -2.76\,\log(P(\text{days})) - 1.4$. So, by measuring the period ($P$), its absolute magnitude ($M_v$) is obtained. By measuring the brightness of the Cepheid through the light that reaches a telescope, the apparent magnitude ($m_v$) is obtained, but both magnitudes ($M_v$ and $m_v$) are related to the distance ($d$) from the star to us, given by: $d = 10^{(m_v - M_v + 5)/5}$ where $d$ is given in parsecs (pc), a unit of distance. For this reason, Cepheids are fundamental in the determination of extragalactic distances. The graph shows the relation between the luminosity of a Cepheid and its radius, on an arbitrary scale, over time (the curves are labelled "Luminosidade" and "Raio", the horizontal axis "Tempo"; small drawings of the star at five instants are shown under the curves).

By analysing the graph, put F (False) or V (True) in front of each of the following statements:
( ) The luminosity of the Cepheid rises rapidly and falls slowly;
( ) When the luminosity of the Cepheid is decreasing it reaches its maximum size;
( ) When the radius of the Cepheid is a minimum, its luminosity is not yet at its maximum;
( ) When the radius of the Cepheid is a maximum, its luminosity is a minimum.

