problems.problem:
(0.2 for each correct answer) The star chart shows the constellation of Orion (white area surrounded by the grey) and its main stars. The horizontal axis represents Right Ascension (RA) and the vertical one represents Declination (Dec.). Both define the equatorial coordinates of the stars and are analogous, respectively, to the geographic coordinates Longitude and Latitude. A positive Declination indicates that the star is in the Northern Celestial Hemisphere (NCH) and a negative one, in the Southern Celestial Hemisphere (SCH). The "size" of the black circles is proportional to the apparent brightness of the object, that is, its apparent magnitude. The magnitude scale is at the bottom of the image. The greater the brightness, the smaller the value of its magnitude.

Put F (False) or V (True) in front of each of the following statements:

( ) The star Rigel is in the Southern Celestial Hemisphere (SCH);
( ) The star Mintaka is brighter than Rigel;
( ) The whole constellation of Orion is in the NCH;
( ) The star Mintaka, one of the Three Marias (the three stars of Orion's Belt), has a Right Ascension close to $5^{\mathrm{h}}\,30^{\mathrm{min}}$;
( ) The Three Marias have apparent magnitudes between 1 and 2.

